What:		/sys/bus/dax/devices/daxX.Y/memmap_on_memory
Date:		January, 2024
KernelVersion:	v6.8
Contact:	nvdimm@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		(RW) Control the memmap_on_memory setting if the dax device
		were to be hotplugged as system memory. This determines whether
		the 'altmap' for the hotplugged memory will be placed on the
		device being hotplugged (memmap_on_memory=1) or if it will be
		placed on regular memory (memmap_on_memory=0). This attribute
		must be set before the device is handed over to the 'kmem'
		driver (i.e.  hotplugged into system-ram). Additionally, this
		depends on CONFIG_MHP_MEMMAP_ON_MEMORY, and a globally enabled
		memmap_on_memory parameter for memory_hotplug. This is
		typically set on the kernel command line -
		memory_hotplug.memmap_on_memory set to 'true' or 'force'."

What:		/sys/bus/dax/devices/daxX.Y/state
Contact:	nvdimm@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		(RW) Controls the state of the memory region.
		Applies to all memory blocks associated with the device.
		Applies to the dax_kmem driver.  When the device's
		"mm_capabilities" is not NODE_MEMORY_CAP_ALL the memory is onlined
		onto a private node (an N_MEMORY node not in N_MEMORY_FALLBACK) whose set bits select which
		mm services may touch it; otherwise it is onlined as ordinary
		system RAM.

		Reading returns the current state; the writable states mirror
		the per-block /sys/devices/system/memory/memoryX/state ABI::

		  "unplugged": memory blocks are not present
		  "online": memory is online, zone chosen by the kernel
		  "online_kernel": memory is online in ZONE_NORMAL
		  "online_movable": memory is online in ZONE_MOVABLE

		"offline" (memory blocks are present but offline) may also be
		reported - this happens when the device is bound while the
		auto_online_blocks policy is "offline".  It cannot be written,
		as it's not useful and creates device destruction races.

		A device can only be onlined from the "unplugged" state, so a
		device must be returned to "unplugged" before it can be onlined
		into a different state.

What:		/sys/bus/dax/devices/daxX.Y/mm_capabilities
Date:		June, 2026
KernelVersion:	v6.21
Contact:	nvdimm@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		(RW) dax_kmem only.  A NODE_MEMORY_CAP_* bitmask selecting how the
		device onlines its memory.  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_ALL (~0, the default)
		onlines it as ordinary system RAM; any other value onlines it as a
		private node (an N_MEMORY node not in N_MEMORY_FALLBACK) whose set bits are the only mm
		services that may act on the node's memory -- everything else is
		isolated.  Accepts decimal or 0x hex.  Writable only while the
		device is "unplugged" (-EBUSY otherwise).

		The capability bits (see include/linux/nodemask.h) are:

		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_RECLAIM	reclaim of the node's folios,
						by the mm and by userspace madvise
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_USER_NUMA	userspace placement and migration
						via mempolicy and move/migrate_pages
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_DEMOTION	reclaim demotion/tiering onto the
						node (requires RECLAIM)
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_NUMA_BALANCING NUMA balancing scan/migrate of
						the node's folios
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_LTPIN		FOLL_LONGTERM GUP pins
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_COLLAPSE	THP collapse (khugepaged/madvise)
		  NODE_MEMORY_CAP_HUGETLB	hugetlb pool allocation

		Dependencies (DEMOTION requires RECLAIM) are validated at hotplug,
		not at write time: an inconsistent mask is accepted by the write
		but the subsequent online fails.

What:		/sys/bus/dax/devices/daxX.Y/dax_file
Date:		June, 2026
KernelVersion:	v6.21
Contact:	nvdimm@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		(RW) dax_kmem only.  When set, mmap()ing the /dev/daxX.Y char
		device yields an ordinary ANONYMOUS mapping whose faults (and
		swap-in) are bound to the device's node, i.e. a way to fault
		anonymous memory onto a specific node through the dax device.
		This is independent of "mm_capabilities": it is a dax-only extension, not
		an mm-service capability, so it is its own toggle rather than a
		per-node opt-in.  When clear (the default) no cdev is installed
		and opening /dev/daxX.Y returns -ENXIO (classic kmem behaviour).
		Writable only while the device is "unplugged" (-EBUSY otherwise).

		Faults are base-page (PAGE_SIZE) only unless the node opts into
		reclaim (the "reclaim" attr), because a node that cannot
		compact/reclaim risks failing higher-order (THP) allocations for
		want of a given order; with reclaim, THP is allowed.

What:		/sys/bus/dax/devices/daxX.Y/adistance
Date:		June, 2026
KernelVersion:	v6.21
Contact:	nvdimm@lists.linux.dev
Description:
		(RW) dax_kmem only.  Present on every dax_kmem device.

		Abstract distance that adjusts the placement of the node in the
		memory-tier hierarchy (larger is a lower tier).

		May only be written while the device is "unplugged".

		No effect if NODE_MEMORY_CAP_DEMOTION is not also selected.
